#' named \code{domain}), with small differences between them: for example,
#' not every context has every property below. \code{go_domain()} checks the
#' values against the contexts it can be used in; when the object is placed
#' in a figure it is checked again against that context's rules.
#'
#' @param arg Optional named list, or graph object of the same type, to
#'   initialise properties from. Named arguments take precedence.
#' @param column If there is a layout grid, use the domain for this column
#'   in the grid for this funnelarea trace. Integer in the interval [0,
#'   Inf]. Not available in every context.
#' @param row If there is a layout grid, use the domain for this row in the
#'   grid for this funnelarea trace. Integer in the interval [0, Inf]. Not
#'   available in every context.
#' @param x Sets the horizontal domain of this funnelarea trace (in plot
#'   fraction). Vector or list of 2 element(s).
#' @param y Sets the vertical domain of this funnelarea trace (in plot
#'   fraction). Vector or list of 2 element(s).
#' @param ... Not used: properties must be named in full.
#' @param skip_invalid If \code{TRUE}, silently drop invalid properties
#'   instead of raising an error.
#' @return A \code{go_domain} object (a named list).
#'
#' @noMd
#' @export
#' @examples
#' go_figure() |>
#'   add_funnelarea(
#'     domain = go_domain()
#'   )
go_domain <- function(
    arg = NULL, ..., column = NULL, row = NULL, x = NULL, y = NULL,
    skip_invalid = FALSE
) {
  shared_object("domain", arg, environment(), list(...), skip_invalid)
}

#' Pattern objects
#'
#' Sets the pattern within the marker. Used for \code{pattern},
#' \code{fillpattern} in 9 places in the schema. \code{go_pattern()} creates
#' one; a named list with the same properties works too.
#'
#' @details
#' These objects appear in 9 places in the plotly.js schema (properties
#' named \code{pattern}, \code{fillpattern}), all with the same properties
#' and rules.
#'
#' @param arg Optional named list, or graph object of the same type, to
#'   initialise properties from. Named arguments take precedence.
#' @param bgcolor When there is no colorscale sets the color of background
#'   pattern fill. Defaults to a `marker.color` background when `fillmode`
#'   is "overlay". Otherwise, defaults to a transparent background. Color:
#'   a CSS color string or R color name, or a vector of these.
#' @param fgcolor When there is no colorscale sets the color of foreground
#'   pattern fill. Defaults to a `marker.color` background when `fillmode`
#'   is "replace". Otherwise, defaults to dark grey or white to increase
#'   contrast with the `bgcolor`. Color: a CSS color string or R color
#'   name, or a vector of these.
#' @param fgopacity Sets the opacity of the foreground pattern fill.
#'   Defaults to a 0.5 when `fillmode` is "overlay". Otherwise, defaults to
#'   1. Number in the interval [0, 1].
#' @param fillmode Determines whether `marker.color` should be used as a
#'   default to `bgcolor` or a `fgcolor`. One of \code{"replace"},
#'   \code{"overlay"}.
#' @param path Sets a custom path for pattern fill. Use with no `shape` or
#'   `solidity`, provide an SVG path string for the regions of the square
#'   from (0,0) to (`size`,`size`) to color. String, or a vector of these.
#' @param shape Sets the shape of the pattern fill. By default, no pattern
#'   is used for filling the area. One of \code{""}, \code{"/"},
#'   \code{"\\\\"}, \code{"x"}, \code{"-"}, \code{"|"}, \code{"+"},
#'   \code{"."}, or a vector of these.
#' @param size Sets the size of unit squares of the pattern fill in pixels,
#'   which corresponds to the interval of repetition of the pattern. Number
#'   in the interval [0, Inf], or a vector of these.
#' @param solidity Sets the solidity of the pattern fill. Solidity is
#'   roughly the fraction of the area filled by the pattern. Solidity of 0
#'   shows only the background color without pattern and solidty of 1 shows
#'   only the foreground color without pattern. Number in the interval [0,
#'   1], or a vector of these.
#' @param ... Not used: properties must be named in full.
#' @param skip_invalid If \code{TRUE}, silently drop invalid properties
#'   instead of raising an error.
#' @return A \code{go_pattern} object (a named list).
#'
#' @noMd
#' @export
#' @examples
#' go_figure() |>
#'   add_bar(
#'     marker = go_bar_marker(
#'       pattern = go_pattern(size = 10)
#'     )
#'   )
go_pattern <- function(
    arg = NULL, ..., bgcolor = NULL, fgcolor = NULL, fgopacity = NULL,
    fillmode = NULL, path = NULL, shape = NULL, size = NULL,
    solidity = NULL, skip_invalid = FALSE
) {
  shared_object("pattern", arg, environment(), list(...), skip_invalid)
}

#' Error bar objects
#'
#' Used for \code{error_x}, \code{error_y}, \code{error_z} in 11 places in
#' the schema. \code{go_error_bar()} creates one; a named list with the same
#' properties works too.
#'
#' @details
#' These objects appear in 11 places in the plotly.js schema (properties
#' named \code{error_x}, \code{error_y}, \code{error_z}), with small
#' differences between them: for example, not every context has every
#' property below. \code{go_error_bar()} checks the values against the
#' contexts it can be used in; when the object is placed in a figure it is
#' checked again against that context's rules.
#'
#' @param arg Optional named list, or graph object of the same type, to
#'   initialise properties from. Named arguments take precedence.
#' @param array Sets the data corresponding the length of each error bar.
#'   Values are plotted relative to the underlying data. Data array: a
#'   vector, factor, Date/POSIXct vector, matrix or list.
#' @param arrayminus Sets the data corresponding the length of each error
#'   bar in the bottom (left) direction for vertical (horizontal) bars
#'   Values are plotted relative to the underlying data. Data array: a
#'   vector, factor, Date/POSIXct vector, matrix or list.
#' @param color Sets the stroke color of the error bars. Color: a CSS color
#'   string or R color name.
#' @param copy_ystyle Logical. Not available in every context.
#' @param copy_zstyle Logical. Not available in every context.
#' @param symmetric Determines whether or not the error bars have the same
#'   length in both direction (top/bottom for vertical bars, left/right for
#'   horizontal bars. Logical.
#' @param thickness Sets the thickness (in px) of the error bars. Number in
#'   the interval [0, Inf].
#' @param traceref Integer in the interval [0, Inf].
#' @param tracerefminus Integer in the interval [0, Inf].
#' @param type Determines the rule used to generate the error bars. If
#'   "constant", the bar lengths are of a constant value. Set this constant
#'   in `value`. If "percent", the bar lengths correspond to a percentage
#'   of underlying data. Set this percentage in `value`. If "sqrt", the bar
#'   lengths correspond to the square of the underlying data. If "data",
#'   the bar lengths are set with data set `array`. One of
#'   \code{"percent"}, \code{"constant"}, \code{"sqrt"}, \code{"data"}.
#' @param value Sets the value of either the percentage (if `type` is set
#'   to "percent") or the constant (if `type` is set to "constant")
#'   corresponding to the lengths of the error bars. Number in the interval
#'   [0, Inf].
#' @param valueminus Sets the value of either the percentage (if `type` is
#'   set to "percent") or the constant (if `type` is set to "constant")
#'   corresponding to the lengths of the error bars in the bottom (left)
#'   direction for vertical (horizontal) bars Number in the interval [0,
#'   Inf].
#' @param visible Determines whether or not this set of error bars is
#'   visible. Logical.
#' @param width Sets the width (in px) of the cross-bar at both ends of the
#'   error bars. Number in the interval [0, Inf].
#' @param ... Not used: properties must be named in full.
#' @param skip_invalid If \code{TRUE}, silently drop invalid properties
#'   instead of raising an error.
#' @return A \code{go_error_bar} object (a named list).
#'
#' @noMd
#' @export
#' @examples
#' go_figure() |>
#'   add_bar(
#'     error_y = go_error_bar(color = "red")
#'   )
go_error_bar <- function(
    arg = NULL, ..., array = NULL, arrayminus = NULL, color = NULL,
    copy_ystyle = NULL, copy_zstyle = NULL, symmetric = NULL,
    thickness = NULL, traceref = NULL, tracerefminus = NULL, type = NULL,
    value = NULL, valueminus = NULL, visible = NULL, width = NULL,
    skip_invalid = FALSE
) {
  shared_object("error_bar", arg, environment(), list(...), skip_invalid)
}

